• I’ll inspect the gate, its addressing checks, existing controls, and the real artifacts before choosing the reach bound.

• The run checker can use gdb’s `$fs_base`/`$gs_base`; LLDB is AArch64-only here, so it has no x86 segment operands to resolve. I’m running the existing artifact gate once to capture the real bounds and TLS displacements before editing.

• The static fix will mirror `PC_REACH`; the run check will add the appropriate segment base instead of discarding the operand. The new controls will verify both the refusal text and that a beyond-reach runtime address is actually counted.

• The first invocation used only the lightweight `gates` shell, which intentionally lacks the LLVM/gdb pins. CI wraps the artifact gate in the default shell from that shell; I’ll use that same nested invocation.

• The measured premise belongs to gate 12’s artifact analysis, not the Lean premise registry. I’ll document it in gate 12’s README entry and keep the report’s existing bounds and schema unchanged.

• Across all 64 real owned-code disassemblies there are no `fs:`/`gs:` operands. I’ll use `TP_REACH = 0x100` (256 bytes): it covers the canary-style `fs:0x28` control with 216 bytes of margin while keeping the exemption narrow.

• The reproducer now exits 1 with the intended refusal, the canary form still reports `bound 0`, and the runtime helper returns and counts the computed `fs_base + 0x100000` address. Next I’m wiring those cases into gate 12 and recording ISS-0028.

• One adjacent case needs the same fail-closed treatment: a segment-qualified `rip` operand must not be mistaken for an ordinary PC-relative access. I’m making segment overrides take precedence, so only register-free `fs`/`gs` operands within `TP_REACH` are accepted.

• The controls, README premise, changelog, and ISS-0028 row are in place. I’m moving to the required bounded checks, then I’ll compare all 64 real stack rows against the baseline.

• `issues.sh` passes all five checks. While the artifact gate runs, I’m reviewing the diff for fail-open paths and checking that no generated files entered the change.

• The full post-fix artifact gate passes, including both reach refusals, the canary bound, the runtime address control, and every pre-existing control. I’m comparing the reports and cleaning only the generated verification outputs before committing.

• All 64 real rows are byte-identical before and after: LLVM remains bound 64 throughout; LCB remains 160, 184, 200, or 216 as in the baseline. The new controls also left all existing control values unchanged.

• Implemented and committed ISS-0028 as `ded66d31f7587062f60d614c4c5f03b075c628af`. The branch is ahead one commit, clean, and was not pushed.

  **Changes**
  - `stack_bound.py`: added `TP_REACH = 0x100` and fail-closed checks for `fs:`/`gs:` operands. Register-relative segment operands are also refused.
  - `stack_access.py`: computes effective addresses using `$fs_base`/`$gs_base` and counts resulting stack accesses.
  - `slice0_artifacts.sh`: added refusal, canary, and runtime-address controls.
  - Updated `Concrete/README.md`, filed `ISS-0028` as closed, and added `changelog.d/2026-11-08-stack-tls-reach.md`.

  **Reach measurement**
  - No `fs:`/`gs:` operands occurred in the 64 real owned-code disassemblies.
  - Chosen bound: 256 bytes, covering `fs:0x28` with 216 bytes of margin.
  - The premise now requires every stack byte to be farther than `TP_REACH` from each segment base in either direction.

  **Verification**
  - Full x86-64 Linux `slice0_artifacts.sh`: passed all 32 examples and controls under the CI-equivalent nested Nix shells, with bounded runs.
  - `fs:[0x100000]` and `gs:[0x100000]`: exit 1 with the reach diagnostic.
  - `fs:[0x28]`: still `bound 0`.
  - Beyond-reach runtime operand: effective address computed and counted.
  - All 64 real stack rows were byte-identical before/after. LLVM bounds remained 64; LCB bounds remained 160/184/200/216.
  - `scripts/checks/issues.sh`: `PASS=5 FAIL=0`.

  Nothing left open.

